hvmd_v1 -- hypervisor configuration in the new hv MD format (content-version 1.0)
=================================================================================

Produces: bin/_test/hvmd_v1/4096/1up-hv.bin

Against: src/_test/pagesize_256m/4096/1up.hdesc  (the config that boots today)

Why
---
The OpenSPARC T2-release hypervisor -- the generation carrying the full LDC API
group, and therefore the whole point of the vdisk/vnet work -- rejects our
historical hv-config outright:

    ABORT: config-basics: MD content-version not found

That format predates the version field.  This variant is the first attempt at
the shape that hv wants, modelled on Sun's own S10image/1c1t/legion-hv.md and
cut down to a single-guest QEMU `-M niagara` domain.

What changed
------------
  - root gains content-version = 0x100000000 (major 1, minor 0) and
    stick-frequency;
  - standard fwd/back arcs replace the named arcs;
  - container nodes appear between root and the leaves: platform, guests, cpus,
    memory, devices, ldc_endpoints, consoles;
  - guest loses inline cpuset/membase/memsize/realbase/uartbase/pdpa and gains
    name ("domain0"), resource_id, mdpa, and arcs to cpu / mblock /
    virtual_devices / channel_devices / console;
  - cpu gains resource_id, gid, partid;
  - memory moves to an mblock node.
  - ldc_endpoints is populated with four ldc_endpoint nodes, decoded out of
    Sun's own legion-hv.md: the guest's control endpoint to the HV/SP
    (target_type 1), a vldc PAIR wired to each other inside the same guest
    (channel 1 <-> 2, target_type 0, target_guest 0), and the hv's own side of
    the control channel carrying svc_id.  A one-domain machine still needs the
    pair, because LDoms always has a server and a client side.  Required
    properties per common/src/reconf.c: channel, target_type, target_channel,
    and target_guest when target_type == 0.

Changes to the guest MD (common.pdesc), which is where the boot was actually won:

  - virtual-devices gains a `fwd ->` per child.  The children already carried
    `back ->`, but arcs are directional: without the forward half OBP's probe
    walks into an empty node, `dev /virtual-devices` succeeds while `ls` prints
    nothing, and every device is "not found".  This is what had kept Solaris
    from booting at all.
  - compatible is written as a LIST everywhere -- mdgen then emits MD_PROP_DATA,
    which is the only form probe.fth reads.
  - root gains content-version = "1.0" (a STRING here; the hv MD's is a u64) and
    md-generation#; platform gains max-cpus; each cpu gains mmu-max-#tsbs.
  - an openboot/{variables,devalias} subtree supplies boot-device = "disk".
  - the console's compatible list is ordered "qcn" FIRST, ahead of
    SUNW,sun4v-console.  That single ordering is what makes console INPUT work:
    Solaris' qcn driver polls the console from a cyclic instead of waiting for
    an interrupt when ddi_binding_name() == "qcn" (illumos qcn.c:376-394), and
    the interrupt it would otherwise wait for does not exist in this hv --
    CONS_TYPE_UART is poll-only, no code path ever posts the console ino to the
    guest.  OBP is unaffected because find-fcode-driver (probe.fth:129) walks
    the whole list and nothing answers to "qcn".
  - a channel-devices subtree, again decoded from Sun's guest MD: channel-devices
    (cnex) hanging off virtual-devices rather than root, a virtual-channel with
    two vldc-ports (hvctl, ldom-primary), a vcc and a vds anchor, plus a
    top-level channel-endpoints container whose endpoints each carry two back
    arcs -- one to the container, one to the port.  tx-ino/rx-ino pairs
    0/1, 2/3, 4/5 match the hv MD's endpoints.

The important choice: no QEMU change is required
------------------------------------------------
Everything the new hv learns about its environment comes from this file:

    mdpa   = 0x1f12000000    NIAGARA_MD_ROM_BASE -- where hw/sparc64/niagara.c
                             already loads 1up-md.bin, so the two-level MD
                             layout costs nothing on the emulator side
    hvuart = 0xfff0c2c000    the hv console the exp #18 QEMU patch models

Status
------
Works.  This pair boots our own build of the T2-release Ontario hypervisor all
the way to a Solaris login prompt under QEMU -M niagara -- first with the 2005
disk.s10hw2 image (exp #18), then with the T2 release's Solaris 10 8/07
disk1.img, which carries the whole LDoms driver stack (exp #19).

Console input works too, confirmed on a boot: with "qcn" first in the console's
compatible list the login prompt responds to the keyboard.

The LDC configuration (both the hv endpoints and the guest channel-devices
subtree) is the newest change and is NOT yet confirmed on a boot.  Everything
else in the lists above is.  The expectation for it is modest by construction:
cnex/vldc/vcc/vds should attach, but no channel can carry traffic yet, because
nothing in this hv raises a guest interrupt.

Full background: docs/niagara-findings.md in the qemu-sparc64 notes repo,
"Track A: what the new hv MD format actually requires".
